94 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(A serendipitous meeting at an olive oil mill, with farmers who have adopted automation over generations while staying in charge of it, gave the author a framework for AI: like the farmers\220 tools, AI should work for humans, never the other way around.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

95 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(From Sicily to Greece, Cuba, China, and Namibia, readers follow the authors\220 encounters with a changing world and see what technology gives and takes. The authors drew on the human story by interviewing people, including arborists, business professionals, IT consultants, and scientists, about their firsthand experiences with AI, both good and bad.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

96 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 71 0 R/T(Amid endless AI headlines, enter: Sicilian olive farmers.)/E(Amid endless AI headlines, enter: Sicilian olive farmers.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

97 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 72 0 R/E(\215We\220re in the early days. AI is becoming ubiquitous but not homogeneous. For most of us, living well in an AI world means using AI well,\216 Dr. Fischer writes. \215This book offers structure for deciding how to use AI, not a manual on tactics. At the heart of the AI era lies the question: What should I do? Ideal readers are curious and uncommitted, not seasoned professionals who\220ve already made up their minds... You Are AI\220s Best Friend means AI serves you, not the other way around. You choose when and how. Your judgment determines success.\216)/Pg 13 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

98 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 72 0 R/T(\204Philip Fischer, PhD)/E(\204Philip Fischer, PhD)/Pg 13 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

99 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 73 0 R/T(Praise)/E(Praise)/Pg 14 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

100 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 73 0 R/E(\215Dr. Philip Fischer zeroes in on the subject of selectively befriending AI in elegant prose that is both thought-provoking and enjoyable for the reader. Having experienced several historical turning point events throughout his life, Dr. Fischer discusses how to navigate through the \217technological earthquake\220 of AI migration we are amid by offering valuable insights, especially into labor market, education, social and economic trends and poses existential questions that are helpful to today\220s society.\216 \204 Mary Dennis Broidy, Former Merrill Lynch FVP Senior Economist, Associate Adjunct Professor, Long Island University)/Pg 14 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

101 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 73 0 R/E<>/Pg 14 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

102 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 73 0 R/E(\215In 19 concise chapters Fischer takes readers from the theoretical origins of \217thinking machines\220 through a discussion of today\220s AI hopes, fears, challenges and opportunities while offering practical advice for workers, educators, policy makers and informed citizens. This book is a useful addition to any reading list focused on AI and its implications for our future way of life.\216 \204 Jeremy E. Plotnick, PhD, Assistant Professor, Costello College of Business, George Mason University)/Pg 14 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

